<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Caspar Lavater</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1741">1741</a>–<a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Caspar Lavater was a Swiss pastor, poet and writer from Zürich. His Essays on Physiognomy of 1775–1778, which claimed to read character from facial features, were famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and drew contributions from the young Goethe. A pastor of St. Peter's church in Zürich, he was deported for a time after protesting against the French occupation and died of a wound received in 1799.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-caspar-lavater.jpg" alt="Johann Caspar Lavater" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: August Friedrich Oelenhainz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Kaspar_Lavater.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
